{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1138058-a-globe-of-darkness-or-around-the-world-in-eighty-nights","id":1138058,"title":"A Globe of Darkness, or Around the World in Eighty Nights","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-06T11:43:20-07:00","id":1138058,"title":"A Globe of Darkness, or Around the World in Eighty Nights","content":"Being a WORLD of darkness, I've decided that I want to expand my setting from the fictional City I've built, to a Continent-spanning web of characters. Obviously i don't plan on making an entire earth's worth of content, but an &quot;international&quot; section in my book of plot hooks might be an interesting idea, especially once my players get high-powered enough that the city can't contain their hijinks.<br /> <br /> That being said, how have my fellow Storytellers filled their worlds, what foreign adventures have they created? I plan on making important figures like Princes and Spirit Courts for the more well known cities around the globe (Tokyo, L.A., Rio de Janeiro, London, etc.) and I'm curious who else has gone globetrotting with monsters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12318-gaius94","name":"Gaius94","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-07T06:41:27-07:00","id":1138307,"title":"","content":"It might be helpful to have NPCs that hail from City B, or spend time in City C etc. Why did they leave? Did they mess up really badly? Were they toppled from a position of power? Did they resign the position to look for something else, pursue a dream/hope/etc? Could they go back, would they want to? Could other come FOR them? Would those people try to hurt them, or try to get them to come back? This gives you ample hooks to have your PCs get involved and move around.<br /> <br /> what splats are you making use of? You could easily give shape to a given city by just deciding what its &quot;hat&quot; is: in City B, Werewolves are the dominant supernaturals, in City C it is Changelings and in City D it is Kindred. E is balanced, F is contested. This basic concept gives you a rough outline to play with.<br /> <br /> If you make use mostly of Vampires, Covenants can easily fill that spot. A city in which the Ordo Dracul has the voice with the most weight will be different in its interactions (among Kindred, with Kine, with other Supernaturals) and structure from a Crone's Paradise or Carthian Democracy (?).<br /> Same thing with Clan: If you have a city that is filled with powerful Daeva, it will have a very different dynamic from a place littered with Nosferatu, or a Ventrue enterprise.<br /> <br /> BUT! Don't limit yourself to a hat. It is a starting point, something to play with, to be able to make quick decisions and allow to improvise and flesh out stuff, not a end-all-be-all. Have the powerful Daeva-city under the rulership of a particularly ugly Nosferatu. The Ventrue Enterprise resides entirely on Gangrel and Mekhet participation, who are aware of their importance.<br /> The Ordo city is politically instable because Dragons just don't pay that much attention to politics, and their devotion to their studies causes problems and discontent with the other Covenants, who might conspire. the Carthian Democracy is actually a farce at the hands of the Invictus, and the crone's paradise is actually one of the most serene and peaceful cities imaginable.<br /> <br /> Decide on means of travelling and responsibilities: a Pack leaving for a while is very different from a Coterie or a Cabal going on a Trip, and might have very different repercussions.<br /> Especially vampires might take great interest in the &quot;how&quot; part of travel, seeing how they are more limited than the others.<br /> <br /> On another note: make sure that, upon return after a longer journey, the world has NOT stopped moving. Describe vividly the changes that have taken place: that one shop closed down, there is a construction site on that road now so you need to take another way to that restaurant, your usually bartender has stopped working and the new guy sucks/is awesome, the season has changed so it is warmer/colder than when you left.<br /> Do not go overboard though, give plenty of stuff that you recognize upon returning with an 'aha! that's still here!' like you would experience after coming home from a long trip. The coffee still smells and tastes just as you remember it, you hairdresser looks just as old as she always did, they painted that one house across the street in a colour that is supposedly one shade brighter, but you'd need scientific instruments to notice the difference etc.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}